course: dictionary
lesson: 5919
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one you need. The word maroon is a good example. «Her maroon jumper matched the school colours.» Here maroon is a colour: a dark brownish-red, like the jumper you wear.
explain: «The sailors were marooned on a desert island for a week.» To maroon someone means to leave them alone in a place they cannot escape from. The word marooned is the clue. Check it by swapping in your meaning: "left alone on an island with no way off" still makes sense.
explain: «Escaped enslaved people formed free maroon settlements in Jamaica and Brazil.» Here maroon is a noun, a name for these communities and the people in them. Look for clues like the word settlements, and ask: does the sentence make sense as a colour?
explain: Keep looking at the words around the word. A maroon can also be a loud firework used as a signal, and the Maroons is the nickname of a Queensland rugby league team. When you are unsure, read the sentence again and ask which meaning fits best.
fact: Maroon is a dark brownish-red colour.
fact: «Marooned» means stuck, left alone in a place you cannot escape from, like a desert island.
fact: «Maroon settlements» were free communities formed by escaped enslaved people in places such as Jamaica, Suriname and Brazil.
fact: Palmares in Brazil was a maroon community that lasted for most of the 1600s.
fact: A maroon is also a loud firework rocket used as a signal, and the Maroons is the nickname of the Queensland rugby league team.
exercise: «Her maroon jumper matched the school colours.» What colour is maroon? | Bright green | Pale blue | Bright yellow | *Dark brownish-red
exercise: «The sailors were marooned on a desert island.» What does marooned mean? | *Left alone where they cannot escape | Given a gift by a friendly king | Painted bright red | Sailing very fast across the sea
exercise: «The Maroons built free communities in the mountains and forests.» Who were the Maroons? | Makers of loud fireworks | A rugby team from Brazil | *People who escaped slavery and built free communities | Soldiers who searched the forests
test: «The fireworks boomed, and a maroon lit up the night sky over the harbour.» What is a maroon in this sentence? | A dark red jumper | *A loud firework used as a signal | A small desert island | A kind of sailing ship
test: Ella says marooned means very happy in a big house. What does marooned really mean? | Running fast around a track | *Stuck, left alone where you cannot escape | Covered in dark red paint | Very happy and relaxed
exam: Read this: «Escaped enslaved people formed free maroon settlements in Jamaica and Brazil.» What does maroon mean here? | A rugby league team from Queensland | A dark brownish-red colour | *A community of people who escaped slavery | A loud firework rocket
